AWS Service Compute Containers:- 
Containers with ECS & EKS these are the main containserization service within aws.

Container Orchestrators:- 
	It is the brains of a containerized environments.
	Responsibilities:- Deploying containers across all available servers.
			   Load Balancing requests to containers.
			   Provide container to container connectivity.
			   Restarting failed containers.
			   Moving containers when hosts failed.
	eg:- Kubernetes, Apache Mesos, ECS etc.

Elastic Container Service(ECS):- 
	Fully managed container orchestration service that helps manage, and scale containarized application.
	It is managed by the AWS. ECS is just a brain. 
	The containers are going to run on EC2 or Fargate another type of serverless offering.

Elastic Kubernetes Service(EKS):- 
	EKS is managed k8s service. It manages control plane. 
	Users are responsible for managing control plane.
	If you use Fargate, then aws also manages worker node.
	Managing the conrolplane / Master node is difficult. Running, Scalling and Securing is difficult. 

  Benefits:- Runs & Scale Control-plane across multiple Availability Zones.
  	     Scale control plane instances based on load. 
	     Can integrate with other Aws services like IAM, Elastic Load Balancer, ECR for container images.

ECS vs EKS:-

ECS is proprietary to aws so migrating to another cloud provider could be difficult.
EKS is K8s open source and can be run on any platform. 
More aws services you configured you cluster to interact with the harder it will be to move to another provider.
ECS has a simpler architecture. Simpler API. Easier to ramp up new team members. 
K8s is very complex and has a steep learning curve. With EKS you have to learn k8s and EKS specific features.
K8a has larger community. More online support, More tooling like Helm,ArgoCD,Kustomize.
ECS pricing- no cost for control plane. Pay for infra and running application(EC2/Fargate,EBS)
EKS Pricing- More Expensive, You have to pay for control plane and worker-nodes/Fargate.

	
